  "updated": "2026-09-15T21:28:00.672666+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Cool and showery conditions are expected from Yaak to Glacier\n   National Park through this evening. \n\n-  Warming and drying trend expected through Wednesday. \n\n-  Shower and Thunderstorm pattern develops Thursday and continues\n   into Saturday. \n\n-  Another warming and drying trend Sunday through early next\n   week.\n\nSatellite imagery this afternoon shows a large upper level low\ndigging southwards from Vancouver Island towards northern\nCalifornia. To the east of this feature, a steady stream of clouds\ncan be seen tracking over northern Idaho into northwest Montana. A\nshortwave trough will slowly track eastwards along the Canadian\nBorder and continue to produce showers through this evening. So \nif you're planning on being outside from Yaak to Eureka to Glacier,\nyou may want to bring a jacket. Despite the cloud cover, \ntemperatures are generally 5 to 10 degrees warmer than they were \nat this time yesterday. Highs will warm into the low 60s across\nnorthwest Montana. Further south the clouds are thinner or\nnonexistent, so expect warmer temperatures rising into the 60s \nand 70s, especially over central Idaho and Lemhi County.\n\nOn Wednesday, the trough moving eastwards towards central Montana\nand induce a 1026 millibar high near Cut Bank. This feature\ncombined with lower pressure over southern Idaho will produce a\nnortheast wind gradient across the region. Generally 10 to 20 mph\nare expected during the day. By the evening time, the gradient\nwill tighten a bit and easterly gusts exceeding 25 mph could be\nfound (70-80% chance) in the channeled areas along and west of the\nContinental Divide. There is a 82% chance that winds could exceed\n30 mph between Salmon and Tendoy along Highway 28 during the\nevening hours. Other than the winds, highs will be near seasonable\nlevels...70s across western Montana, and 80s in Idaho. \n\nOn Thursday the upper low that we mentioned in the beginning will\nstart to wobble northeastwards towards southwest southern Idaho.\nIt will draw up over 200 percent of normal, monsoon moisture from\nthe Four Corners region. Wind shear has trended to be stronger as\nthis system could bring a 45 knot mid-level jet streak by Thursday\nnight. This means that not only will any thunderstorms produce a \nlot of rain, they could contain hail. Initially guidance is\nshowing less than 300 J/KG CAPE/instability in the hail growth\nzone on Thursday which could support small hail. On Friday,\ndepending on how much heating we're able to get, the hail\npotential seems to be greater with the GFS showing much higher\ninstability values that could support hail to one inch or greater.\nLightning, gusty winds will be other threats with the storms. The\nNBM, ensemble and AI guidance are depicting the area from central\nIdaho east and north including west-central and southwest Montana\nas being the areas with the highest probability for thunderstorms. \n\nAs the low pulls away from the region on Saturday, the focus for\nshowers and thunderstorms shifts eastwards from US-93 corridor\neastwards into central Montana. We could have lingering clouds \nover central Idaho but the lower valleys should remain mostly dry\nwith moderating temperatures. \n\nModel guidance continue to show large upper ridging developing\nSunday into early next week. We could have patchy fog developing\nalong river bottoms each morning. If you take the full potential,\nhighs could crack the the 80 degree mark in Missoula, which would\nbe around 10 degrees above average. The highest probabilities for\n80s are in the lower valleys of Idaho (80-90%). This ridge would\nbring us back to summer-like conditions for 2-3 days. There are \nhints that there could be a frontal system that could temper \nthings a bit by mid- week.",
